Title:
Perfect Loader — “Perfect” In-Memory DLL Loader for Windows (native loader redirection)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Perfect Loader is a reference implementation for loading Windows DLLs directly from memory by redirecting the native `LoadLibrary` path rather than reimplementing a custom PE loader. The goal is “feature parity” with the Windows loader by forcing it to consume in-memory/modified backing data via NTAPI-level interception and section tricks. It provides three loading approaches: hook-based redirection around `NtMapViewOfSection` (and additional hooks for Windows 11 24H2 changes), “Module Doppelgänging” using NTFS transactions to create a section from transacted content, and module/section hollowing by hooking `NtCreateSection`/`NtMapViewOfSection`. The project includes multiple hook mechanisms (detours, hardware breakpoints, process instrumentation callbacks) and optional stealthy post-processing (unlinking from loader lists, removing headers, notifications, VEH, etc.). It’s primarily useful for red teams, implant developers, and security researchers studying loader internals and in-memory module tradecraft, and secondarily for blue teams to understand detection surfaces around section mapping and loader tampering.

Technical Focus:
- Native loader redirection vs manual PE loader reimplementation (`LoadLibrary` parity)
- NTAPI hooking: `NtMapViewOfSection`, `NtCreateSection`, (optionally `NtOpenFile`)
- Module/section hollowing and section-backed image mapping manipulation
- Transactional NTFS (TxF) concepts applied to “Module Doppelgänging”
- Hook delivery methods: detours, hardware breakpoints, process instrumentation callbacks
- Stealth options: PEB/Ldr list unlinking, header wiping, notification/VEH/PI callback removal

Use Cases:
- Load a DLL from memory while leveraging the real Windows loader behavior
- Research and prototype loader-evasion and section-mapping tradecraft
- Build test harnesses to evaluate EDR telemetry around section creation/mapping and loader callbacks
- Compare resilience of different in-memory loading strategies across Windows versions (incl. Win11 24H2)
- Develop defensive detections for anomalous `NtCreateSection`/`NtMapViewOfSection` patterns and loader list tampering

Keywords:
Windows loader, LoadLibrary, in-memory DLL loading, reflective loading, manual mapping, NtMapViewOfSection, NtCreateSection, NtOpenFile, section object, image mapping, module hollowing, section hollowing, Process Doppelgänging, Module Doppelgänging, NTFS transactions, TxF, detour patching, hardware breakpoints, process instrumentation callback, PEB, Ldr lists, vectored exception handler, Windows 11 24H2